  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「リーキ (種)」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%AA%E3%83%BC%E3%82%AD_(%E7%A8%AE)",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 106388465,
      "text": "リーキ は、ネギ属の一種である。この野生種は英語では「wild leek（野生のリーキ）」または「broadleaf wild leek（葉が広い野生のリーキ）」と通常呼ばれている。自生地域は南ヨーロッパから西アジアであるが、その他多くの場所で栽培され、多くの国々で帰化している。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Allium ampeloprasum」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allium_ampeloprasum",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1377819548,
      "text": "Allium ampeloprasum is a member of the onion genus Allium. The wild plant is commonly known as wild leek or broadleaf wild leek. Its native range includes southern Europe, southwestern Asia and North Africa, but it has been cultivated and naturalized in many other countries."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Allium ampeloprasum」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allium_ampeloprasum#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1377819548,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Allium ampeloprasum is the source of several vegetables, most notably:\n\nLeek (var. porrum)\nElephant garlic or great-headed garlic (var. ampeloprasum)\nPearl onion (var. sectivum)\nKurrat (var. kurrat), Egyptian leek or salad leek. This variety has small bulbs, and primarily the leaves are eaten.\nPersian leek (A. ampeloprasum ssp. persicum). A cultivated allium native to the Middle East and Iran, grown for culinary purposes and called tareh in Persian. The linear green leaves have a mild onion flavor and are eaten raw, either alone, or in food combinations.\nSome sources (especially archeological ones) refer to each of these as a separate species, but they are now united as A. ampeloprasum.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
